1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,542
Today we check whether a data centre rides through a fault
in the grid, in open-source Electrisim.

2
00:00:06,592 --> 00:00:10,418
Large data centres draw their power through UPS rectifiers.

3
00:00:10,468 --> 00:00:18,172
When the voltage dips, the UPS moves the servers to its battery,
and the grid suddenly loses hundreds of megawatts.

4
00:00:18,222 --> 00:00:21,344
Grid operators now ask large loads to ride through:

5
00:00:21,394 --> 00:00:24,164
stay connected, and get their power back quickly.

6
00:00:24,214 --> 00:00:32,270
We will size a STATCOM for the reactive current a dip asks
for, and simulate the UPS in the time domain.

7
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:33,754
Six steps.

8
00:00:33,804 --> 00:00:34,781
Open the template.

9
00:00:34,831 --> 00:00:38,202
Look at the data centre and its UPS.

10
00:00:38,252 --> 00:00:39,571
Look at the STATCOM.

11
00:00:39,621 --> 00:00:41,624
Size the STATCOM for fault ride-through.

12
00:00:41,674 --> 00:00:44,703
Simulate a remote fault, with and without the STATCOM.

13
00:00:44,753 --> 00:00:50,520
And finally a close-in fault, where we tune the
UPS until the data centre passes.

14
00:00:51,370 --> 00:00:52,153
Step one.

15
00:00:52,203 --> 00:00:57,985
Open Electrisim in the browser, go to File,
New, and search for data centre.

16
00:00:58,035 --> 00:01:00,901
The template is in the Tutorials group:

17
00:01:00,951 --> 00:01:02,568
data centre fault ride-through.

18
00:01:02,618 --> 00:01:03,401
Click Create.

19
00:01:03,451 --> 00:01:12,149
A 138 kilovolt utility grid with 8000 MVA of short-circuit power
feeds the point of interconnection over two lines.

20
00:01:12,199 --> 00:01:24,230
A 400 MVA transformer steps down to the 34.5 kilovolt campus bus, with the 300
megawatt data centre and a STATCOM rated plus and minus 150 Mvar.

21
00:01:25,085 --> 00:01:26,808
Step two, the data centre.

22
00:01:26,858 --> 00:01:29,999
Double-click the load and open the Data center tab.

23
00:01:30,050 --> 00:01:32,482
The computational load model is switched on.

24
00:01:32,532 --> 00:01:39,220
85 percent of the load is IT equipment behind
the UPS, and the rest is cooling.

25
00:01:39,270 --> 00:01:44,894
Below 0.85 per unit the UPS moves the IT load to its battery.

26
00:01:44,944 --> 00:01:54,824
It goes back to the grid once the voltage has been above 0.9 per unit for one
second, and then walks the load back in over five seconds.

27
00:01:54,874 --> 00:01:55,888
The requirement says:

28
00:01:55,938 --> 00:02:01,562
after the voltage has recovered, the power must be
back at 90 percent within one second.

29
00:02:01,612 --> 00:02:03,690
And this is the ride-through curve:

30
00:02:03,740 --> 00:02:07,946
zero volts for 150 milliseconds, then
0.7, 0.85 and 0.9 per unit.

31
00:02:07,996 --> 00:02:13,265
As long as the voltage stays above this curve,
the data centre must ride through.

32
00:02:14,118 --> 00:02:15,489
Step three, the STATCOM.

33
00:02:15,539 --> 00:02:19,752
On its Dynamics tab, the STATCOM is a
converter without active power.

34
00:02:19,802 --> 00:02:24,371
It regulates the bus voltage, and in a dip
it adds reactive current:

35
00:02:24,421 --> 00:02:31,121
a k factor of 2 times the voltage drop beyond a 10
percent deadband, up to its current limit.

36
00:02:31,171 --> 00:02:32,542
Run a load flow.

37
00:02:32,592 --> 00:02:39,648
The STATCOM already supplies about 63 Mvar for the data
centre and holds the campus bus at 1.0 per unit.

38
00:02:44,016 --> 00:02:45,863
Step four, size the STATCOM.

39
00:02:45,913 --> 00:02:48,139
Go to Simulate, Grid-Forming STATCOM Sizing.

40
00:02:48,189 --> 00:02:55,346
Select the STATCOM, choose the data centre load as the
facility, and the requirement set for a data centre.

41
00:02:55,396 --> 00:03:03,312
Its fault ride-through duty asks for additional reactive current, k times
the voltage drop, on the rated current of the facility.

42
00:03:03,362 --> 00:03:08,244
For the deepest dip, to zero, that is limited to one per unit.

43
00:03:08,294 --> 00:03:09,002
Click Calculate.

44
00:03:09,052 --> 00:03:18,106
The data centre needs no supercapacitors for this, but the converter
must carry 306 MVA of reactive current, about 5 kA.

45
00:03:18,156 --> 00:03:22,659
Our 150 MVA STATCOM is too small for that requirement:

46
00:03:22,709 --> 00:03:26,073
it covers dips down to about 0.86 per unit.

47
00:03:26,123 --> 00:03:29,866
That is the size to discuss with the grid operator.

48
00:03:30,718 --> 00:03:32,504
Step five, the time domain.

49
00:03:32,554 --> 00:03:34,340
Go to Simulate, Transient Stability.

50
00:03:34,390 --> 00:03:36,543
Set 60 hertz and 10 seconds.

51
00:03:36,593 --> 00:03:47,560
Put a fault on the 138 kilovolt utility bus from one second to 1.15 seconds, through
a reactance of 0.1 per unit, like a fault further away in the grid.

52
00:03:47,610 --> 00:03:52,334
Choose the point of interconnection as the POI bus, and run.

53
00:03:52,384 --> 00:03:54,170
The verdict is a pass.

54
00:03:54,220 --> 00:04:06,288
The campus voltage dips to about 0.88 per unit, above the UPS transfer voltage,
so the UPS stays on the grid and the IT load never leaves it.

55
00:04:08,928 --> 00:04:13,867
Now take the STATCOM out of service and
run the same fault again.

56
00:04:13,917 --> 00:04:19,623
Without it, the campus voltage starts lower, at
0.97 per unit, and dips to 0.84.

57
00:04:19,673 --> 00:04:22,309
That is just below the transfer voltage.

58
00:04:22,359 --> 00:04:30,751
The UPS moves 257 megawatts to its battery, waits one second,
and walks the load back in over five seconds.

59
00:04:30,801 --> 00:04:34,205
The power is back 5.5 seconds after the voltage.

60
00:04:34,255 --> 00:04:35,740
The verdict is fail:

61
00:04:35,790 --> 00:04:37,274
power back too late.

62
00:04:37,324 --> 00:04:43,798
So even a 150 Mvar STATCOM keeps this data centre
on the grid in a remote fault.

63
00:04:46,414 --> 00:04:48,235
Step six, a close-in fault.

64
00:04:48,285 --> 00:04:55,343
Put the STATCOM back in service, and move the fault to the
point of interconnection, as a bolted fault.

65
00:04:55,393 --> 00:04:58,336
Now the voltage is zero for 150 milliseconds.

66
00:04:58,386 --> 00:05:06,192
That is inside the ride-through curve, but no load can draw
power at zero volts, so every UPS transfers.

67
00:05:06,242 --> 00:05:11,056
With the default settings the power comes back
5.5 seconds after the voltage.

68
00:05:11,106 --> 00:05:11,430
Fail.

69
00:05:11,480 --> 00:05:18,164
The STATCOM gives its full reactive current in the dip and
regulates the voltage when the load drops.

70
00:05:20,778 --> 00:05:23,398
The fix is in the UPS.

71
00:05:23,448 --> 00:05:30,406
Open the data centre load again, and set the return delay to
0.2 seconds and the walk-in to half a second.

72
00:05:30,456 --> 00:05:38,082
A shorter return delay brings the UPS back to the grid sooner,
and a short walk-in ramps the servers up quickly.

73
00:05:38,132 --> 00:05:42,087
Check with the UPS supplier that its rectifier allows this.

74
00:05:42,137 --> 00:05:43,422
Run the same fault.

75
00:05:43,472 --> 00:05:48,762
The power is back 0.7 seconds after the voltage,
and the verdict is now a pass.

76
00:05:48,812 --> 00:05:56,438
The chart shows the power the data centre draws from the grid,
and Export CSV saves every curve for your report.

77
00:05:57,587 --> 00:06:01,213
That is fault ride-through of a data centre in Electrisim:

78
00:06:01,263 --> 00:06:09,666
a STATCOM sized for the k factor reactive current, and the UPS
simulated in the time domain, with a clear verdict.

79
00:06:09,716 --> 00:06:14,444
The template is free in File, New, and the
documentation explains every setting.

80
00:06:14,494 --> 00:06:15,547
Thanks for watching.

